{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"scenario_analysis (by Lead Analyst):
{
 "scenarios": [
  {
   "case": "Bull",
   "probability": "25%",
   "driver": "AI-device upgrade cycle extends and the foldable iPhone Duo opens a new price tier; Services holds double-digit growth with advertising and cloud replacing lost commission; gross margin holds near 49% despite memory costs; the multiple stays at 40x on FY2027E EPS of $10.10.",
   "evidence": "June-quarter revenue rose 16.4% with double-digit growth in iPhone, Mac and Services and in every geographic segment; Greater China rebounded 22.4% to $18.8B; R&D rose 32.2% to $11.7B; Apple took 65% of the $600+ premium tier and raised unit share 3.9 points to 20.2% [S6, S47, S77, S79].",
   "outcome": "$404 a share, +15.8% from $348.91 before the 0.31% dividend; 40x FY2027E EPS of $10.10.",
   "implication": "Own it: the watchlist rating converts to an add only once the price is justified by delivered growth rather than by the multiple; at this probability the expected contribution is +4.0% to the weighted return."
  },
  {
   "case": "Base",
   "probability": "45%",
   "driver": "Growth decelerates to the consensus FY2027 figure of 10.3% revenue and 8.1% EPS, gross margin normalises to about 48% once the tariff refunds wash out, regulation trims rather than removes commission, and the multiple drifts back to the FY2025 year-end 34x.",
   "evidence": "Nine-month actuals imply a September quarter of $113.3B and $1.96 of EPS, +10.6% and +6.5%, against +16.4% and +29% in June; consensus FY2027 revenue $526.79B and EPS $9.56; the June-quarter 50.1% gross margin included about 2 points of tariff refunds, so the underlying figure was 48.1% [S93, S6].",
   "outcome": "$325 a share, -6.9% from $348.91; 34x FY2027E EPS of $9.56.",
   "implication": "Hold on the watchlist with no position: the business compounds at 8-10% a year, but at $348.91 the buyer is paying for the bull case and receiving the base case."
  },
  {
   "case": "Bear",
   "probability": "30%",
   "driver": "Memory costs compress hardware gross margin while the App Store take rate settles toward the mid-teens and the Google payments are curtailed, so FY2027 EPS falls to $8.60 and the multiple mean-reverts to the FY2023 26x; Samsung converts memory profits into handset price competition and Huawei caps China share.",
   "evidence": "The 10-Q warns memory and storage costs may not be offset by price increases; the EU's Article 6(4) case carries a 10% of sales cap and the €500m fine on 2025-04-23 established the principle; the General Court upheld gatekeeper status on 2026-07-08; Korean and UK regulators are converging on mid-teens commissions; the shares traded at $243.42 within the past year, 30.2% below today's close [S6, S134, S135, S136, S132, S7].",
   "outcome": "$224 a share, -35.8% from $348.91; 26x FY2027E EPS of $8.60.",
   "implication": "Avoid adding at any price above the entry band; the bear case is a 26x multiple on a smaller EPS, and the trigger to re-underwrite is a margin print below 47% or a final EU remedy that removes commission retroactively."
  }
 ],
 "assumptions": [
  {
   "assumption": "Gross margin holds at or above 48% excluding tariff refunds through FY2027, against memory and storage costs the company says will intensify",
   "confidence": "medium-low",
   "disproof": "A December-2026 quarter gross margin below 47% on a like-for-like basis, or iPhone price increases that do not stick",
   "impact": "Each 100bp of gross margin is $5.0bn of operating income, 3.1% of base-case net income and roughly $0.30 of EPS; a return to the FY2025 46.9% margin removes about 11% of FY2027 EPS",
   "early_warning": "DRAM and NAND contract pricing, Samsung's memory-division margin, the spread between Apple's ASP and BOM cost"
  },
  {
   "assumption": "The App Store take rate settles in a 15-26% band rather than collapsing, and Google search-distribution payments inside Services survive in some form",
   "confidence": "medium",
   "disproof": "A final EU Article 6(4) remedy imposing structural separation, or a US order terminating the search-distribution payments",
   "impact": "A 10% cut in Services gross profit is $8.2bn, 6.2% of operating income; App Store commission plus Google payments could exceed a quarter of Services gross profit, which would turn a sentiment issue into an earnings event",
   "early_warning": "Article 6(4) decision, the Supreme Court's ruling on the Epic contempt standard, the DOJ settlement terms, Google's remedy compliance filings"
  },
  {
   "assumption": "Services grows at double digits (12.3% CAGR to $120.5bn trailing) and iPhone premium mix and China share hold near 18%",
   "confidence": "medium",
   "disproof": "Services growth below 8% for two quarters, or Greater China unit share back below 15% as IDC expects the second-half China market to fall 20%",
   "impact": "A 300bp shortfall in Services growth removes roughly $3.6bn of 76%-margin revenue, about 2.2% of operating income; a China reversal hits 17.8% of nine-month revenue",
   "early_warning": "Quarterly Services growth, IDC China share for Q4 2026, advertising and cloud revenue lines replacing lost commission"
  },
  {
   "assumption": "The exit multiple is 34x FY2027E EPS of $9.56, i.e. the FY2025 year-end level rather than the current 40.0x",
   "confidence": "medium",
   "disproof": "A ten-year Treasury yield falling materially below 4%, or a re-acceleration that lifts consensus FY2028 EPS above $11",
   "impact": "Ten points of exit multiple move two-year total return by about 30 percentage points: -10.7% at 30x, +1.2% at 34x, +18.9% at 40x",
   "early_warning": "Ten-year yield and the equity-risk premium, the 50-day versus 200-day average, sell-side target dispersion"
  },
  {
   "assumption": "The buyback continues at roughly 1.6% of shares a year and the FY2026 fourth quarter lands near consensus of $113.3bn of revenue and $1.96 of EPS",
   "confidence": "medium-high",
   "disproof": "A materially smaller May 2027 authorisation than the $100bn of May 2025, or a September-quarter revenue miss",
   "impact": "Halving the buyback removes about 0.8 points of annual EPS growth; a 5% revenue miss on FY2027 consensus removes about $2.30 of EPS at a 48% gross margin",
   "early_warning": "The 2026-11-02 print, the May 2027 authorisation, buyback pace in the FY2026 10-K"
  }
 ],
 "consensus": {
  "consensus": "The market treats Apple as the highest-quality cash compounder in mega-cap technology and pays for it: 40.0x trailing earnings, above every fiscal year-end multiple since FY2022, on the view that Services mix, the buyback and a record premium share justify a permanent premium to Microsoft at 27.7x and Alphabet at 14.8x.",
  "our_view": "The quality is consensus and broadly correct; the edge is narrower and is about price rather than business. The share price capitalises a second growth engine - AI devices, Services, new categories - that no filing evidences, while the 2026 acceleration came from hardware mix, memory-driven competitor retreat and about 2 points of one-off tariff refunds. At $348.91 the probability-weighted outcome is $314, about -10%, so the thesis is not that Apple is a bad business but that it is a good business with no margin of safety, and the watchlist action is a discipline rather than a rejection.",
  "evidence": "The 43-analyst consensus target of $332.39 sits 4.7% below the close and Sell plus Strong Strong Sell ratings rose from one to six between May and October 2026 while the shares gained 28.6%; the reverse DCF needs 15% free-cash-flow growth for a decade at a 9.5% WACC, 2.7 times the 5.5% revenue compound from FY2021 to FY2026E; and half of the five-year 134.2% return came from multiple expansion rather than earnings [S93, S7, S6].",
  "proof_event": "The FY2026 fourth-quarter print on 2026-11-02 and the December-2026 quarter that follows it: if revenue grows more than 10% with gross margin above 48% excluding refunds, the consensus view is right and the multiple is defensible; if margin or growth disappoints, the de-rating case is proved."
 },
 "one_question": {
  "question": "What share of Services revenue and gross profit comes from App Store commissions and from Google's search-distribution payments - the two cash flows that the EU, US courts and the DOJ are directly repricing?",
  "why": "This single undisclosed number decides whether platform regulation is a sentiment issue or an earnings event. Services is $109.2bn of FY2025 revenue at a 75.4% gross margin, $82.3bn of gross profit and 42.2% of the total; a tenth of that pool lost to remedies is $8.2bn, 6.2% of operating income, which would move the bear case from a multiple event to an earnings event. If the exposed share is below a tenth of Services gross profit, the 40x multiple is a rate-and-growth argument and can be defended; if a quarter or more, the base case EPS of $9.56 is overstated and the entry band falls below $287. Apple does not disclose App Store revenue, take rates or the Google payment, so the answer has to come from the PACER exhibits already requested and from the Article 6(4) decision, and no analyst estimate in the register resolves it."
 },
 "modules": {
  "analogs": "Nearest analog: Microsoft 2014-2019. A maturing franchise with a high-margin annuity attached (Office to Office 365) re-rated only after the annuity's growth was demonstrated for several quarters, not on the announcement; Apple's Services is the same mechanism but its growth is being repriced by courts rather than competitors. Cautionary analog: Cisco 2000-2002, a hardware leader priced at a peak multiple on the assumption that the next cycle would be like the last, where the multiple halved while revenue kept growing. Second cautionary analog: IBM 2011-2013, where buybacks at high multiples slowed and the share count could no longer carry EPS growth. Base rate from Apple's own history: the multiple fell from 37.5x to 27.9x between FY2024 and FY2023 year-ends while the business did not deteriorate, which is the mean-reversion the base and bear cases assume [S93, S6].",
  "capital_efficiency": "Capex of $10,041M trailing, 2.2% of revenue, against $135,921M at Microsoft and $159,804M at Alphabet, gives trailing free cash flow of $136,683M at 1.06x net income and a capital-light model that funds $82,226M of buybacks and $15,640M of dividends from operations rather than from the balance sheet. The efficiency is real and is the base case's foundation. The cost is that the capability inputs are bought rather than built: R&D up 32.5% to $34,035M and $13,270M of gross intangibles added with no acquisition line, against a Gemini licence that converts model development into an operating expense. If AI becomes the upgrade reason rather than a feature, the 2%-of-sales capex line is the assumption that breaks first.",
