 "industry_map": "Consumer electronics and platform-services value chain: upstream semiconductors and components (TSMC leading-edge SoCs; memory, displays, cameras, RF from Korea/Japan/US) → assembly and test (Foxconn, Luxshare, Tata in India; concentrated in China with an India ramp) → distribution (carriers, retailers, direct online) → installed base of active devices (about 2.3bn, to verify) → monetization through hardware plus services (App Store, iCloud, advertising, Apple Pay, subscriptions, licensing) → replacement and upgrade cycle. Decisive bottleneck: control of the silicon/OS/developer ecosystem at the premium end, which sustains pricing and the installed-base annuity; binding constraints are advanced-node supply and China/India manufacturing geopolitics, plus platform-regulation limits on services take rates. Apple's place: the integrator with pricing power at the premium end and the largest share of global handset profit pools, monetizing the installed base through services.",
 "geo_lens": "Material. Home and revenue market: United States (largest revenue region); Greater China is the second-largest and most policy-exposed region (roughly 17-20% of revenue in recent years, exact share to be verified from the latest 10-K); Europe, Japan and rest of Asia-Pacific secondary. Supply chain: assembly concentrated in China and India (multi-year India ramp), leading-edge silicon from Taiwan (TSMC), memory and components from Korea/Japan/US. Capital market and listing: Nasdaq (US); comparables include US mega-cap platforms and global handset makers. Report written from a US long-only investor's perspective.",
 "policy_lens": "Material. Jurisdictions to map: United States (DOJ antitrust case against Apple, App Store remedy risk, tariffs and China trade policy, export controls), European Union (Digital Markets Act and App Store/alternative-payment obligations, antitrust), China (SAMR, data-security and local-content rules; revenue market and assembly base), India (production-linked incentives and manufacturing ramp), plus Japan/Korea competition authorities. Policy affects App Store take rate, services margin, supply-chain geography and China demand; price a regulatory haircut to services rather than a binary outcome.",
